Text latex:
  A square $S$ has source and target horizontal maps, left and right vertical
  maps, and evidence that the two composites agree. Its quotient map is the
  map between cokernels induced by the right vertical map.
  For an inclusion of topological pairs, the four corners are the singular chains
  on the relative and ambient subsets of the source and target pairs.

Result quotient square commutes:
  Attribution: topology; cokernel.map; adapted
  Let K: Type
  Let field: FieldStructure(K)
  Let C,D,E,F: ModuleChainComplex(K,field)
  Let S: ChainSquare(K,field,C,D,E,F)
  Conclude:
    compose chain maps(field,D,chain cokernel(field,C,D,S.source),chain cokernel(field,E,F,S.target),
      square quotient map(field,C,D,E,F,S),chain cokernel projection(field,C,D,S.source)) =
    compose chain maps(field,D,F,chain cokernel(field,E,F,S.target),
      chain cokernel projection(field,E,F,S.target),S.right)

Result pair inclusion induces a chain square:
  Attribution: topology; singular chain functor applied to pair inclusions; original
  Let K: Type
  Let field: FieldStructure(K)
  Let H: HomologyTheory(K,field)
  Let X: TopologicalSpace
  Let Q: PairInclusion(X)
  Conclude:
    there exists (S: PairChainSquare(K,field,H,X,Q), square represents pair inclusion(field,H,X,Q,S))

